            <ArticleTitle>Mucus plugging as a cause of acute lobar overdistension.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>38-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Acute lobar overdistension in children is usually indicative of foreign body aspiration, especially when a history of a choking episode is recalled. An unusual presentation of asthma as recurrent overdistension of the left lung in a toddler is described. The child had undergone two consecutive negative bronchoscopies with a presumptive diagnosis of foreign body aspiration; however, it was only the antiasthmatic treatment that resulted in an excellent outcome.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
